/**
 * AI action registry — the NEVER deny-list.
 *
 * Generated by slice S1 of the TManage AI connector (MCP). See
 * `MCP-CONNECTOR-PLAN-2026-10-20.md` §2, §7 (S1) and
 * `MCP-CONNECTOR-ARCHITECTURE-2026-10-20.md` B3 / T-REG-01.
 *
 * This is the route *inventory and classification skeleton*. Every one of the
 * 710 live API routes is accounted for exactly once: either as an op here or as
 * a `NEVER` deny-list entry. `registry-sync.test.ts` enumerates the live routes
 * by reflection and fails if any route is unclassified, double-classified, or
 * carries a capability that disagrees with its op. Slices S2–S4 add the zod
 * input/output schemas, redaction profiles, titles, descriptions and examples
 * to each op; this slice fixes only id, method, path, capability, risk and scope.
 */
import type { NeverEntry, RouteKey } from './types.js';
import { routeKey } from './types.js';

/**
 * Routes the AI connector never exposes, with the reason each is withheld (plan §2):
 * authentication, sessions, MFA and passwords; personal access tokens and the `/mcp`
 * endpoint itself; Google auth; setup, sign-up and the public site; platform admin and
 * account-scoped routes that are not org-bound; invitation accept/register and org creation;
 * the public upload-part route; `org.security.manage` (IP allowlist, security alerts); admin
 * security settings and audit-retention policy; role and policy *definitions*; automation
 * variables, webhooks and app connections (they hold secrets); and billing subscription and
 * seat *changes*. Being in this list is the only sanctioned alternative to being an op: the
 * sync test treats any live route that is neither as an error.
 */
